!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/class2/schema/invalid/a-flatten-directive-names-no-type-expected.tn"
!!schema:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/schema-sidecar.tn"
{
  spec: "[TSON-SCHEMA] §6, [TSON-SCHEMA] §7.2, [TSON-JSON] §6.1.1"
  description: """
    No annotation designates a field an encoding should flatten. A record is closed under its type
    (§7.2) in every encoding, and a schema carrying open-ended data declares a map-typed field, which
    is written the same way wherever it is written -- the entries are map keys, so they are data
    rather than names and no identifier rule reaches them.

    The governing meta declares no type named `rest`, so this is the ordinary unresolved-name error of
    §3.3.3. The mark it retired was a representation directive whose class could not be stated: §6
    confines a directive's force to the encodings in its class and offers the text class as the
    example, and this one bound JSON while TSON text declined it, leaving "the encodings that claim
    it" as the only class it had.

    What the vector guards is that the document fails at all. A processor keeping the retired
    declaration would resolve the name and park it in the author-annotation channel, where it changes
    nothing -- leaving a schema that reads as though it had asked for a flatten no encoding performs.
    """
  meta: "meta.tn"
  import: ["core.tn"]
  error: {
    category: resolver
  }
}
